          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Expression Levels of All the Genes of an Organism Can Be Determined 37  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Arabidopsis Has Been Chosen as a Model Plant 38  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The World of Animal Cells Is Mainly Represented by a Worm, a Fly, a Fish, a Mouse, and a Human 38  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Studies in the Fruit Fly Drosophila Provide a Key to Vertebrate Development 39  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Frog and the Zebrafish Provide Highly Accessible Vertebrate Models 40  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Mouse Is the Predominant Mammalian Model Organism 41  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The COVID-19 Pandemic Has Focused Scientists on the SARS-CoV-2 Coronavirus 42  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Humans Are Unique in Reporting on Their Own Peculiarities 44  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "To Understand Cells and Organisms Will Require Mathematics, Computers, and Quantitative Information 44  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 45  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 46  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 47  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chapter 2 Cell Chemistry and Bioenergetics 49  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE CHEMICAL COMPONENTS OF A CELL 49  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Water Is Held Together by Hydrogen Bonds 50  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Four Types of Noncovalent Attractions Help Bring Molecules Together in Cells 51  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Polar Molecules Form Acids and Bases in Water 52  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Cell Is Formed from Carbon Compounds 53  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Contain Four Major Families of Small Organic Molecules 53  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Chemistry of Cells Is Dominated by Macromolecules with Remarkable Properties 54  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Noncovalent Bonds Specify Both the Precise Shape of a Macromolecule and Its Binding to Other Molecules 55  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 56  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CATALYSIS AND THE USE OF ENERGY BY CELLS 57  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cell Metabolism Is Organized by Enzymes 57  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Biological Order Is Made Possible by the Release of Heat Energy from Cells 58  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Obtain Energy by the Oxidation of Organic Molecules 61  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Oxidation and Reduction Involve Electron Transfers 62  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Enzymes Lower the Activation-Energy Barriers That Block Chemical Reactions 63  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Enzymes Can Drive Substrate Molecules Along Specific Reaction Pathways 64  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "How Enzymes Find Their Substrates: The Enormous Rapidity of Molecular Motions 65  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Free-Energy Change for a Reaction, "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\Delta G"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": ", Determines Whether It Can Occur Spontaneously 66  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Concentration of Reactants Influences the Free-Energy Change and a Reaction's Direction 67  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Standard Free-Energy Change, "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\Delta G^{\\circ}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": ", Makes It Possible to Compare the Energetics of Different Reactions 67  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Equilibrium Constant and "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\Delta G^{\\circ}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Are Readily Derived from Each Other 68  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Free-Energy Changes of Coupled Reactions Are Additive 69  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Activated Carrier Molecules Are Essential for Biosynthesis 69  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Formation of an Activated Carrier Is Coupled to an Energetically Favorable Reaction 70  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "ATP Is the Most Widely Used Activated Carrier Molecule 71  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Energy Stored in ATP Is Often Harnessed to Join Two Molecules Together 72"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
