            0.931
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chapter 1 Cells, Genomes, and the Diversity of Life 1   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE UNIVERSAL FEATURES OF LIFE ON EARTH 2   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Cells Store Their Hereditary Information in the Form of Double-Strand DNA Molecules 2   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Cells Replicate Their Hereditary Information by Templated Polymerization 3   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Cells Transcribe Portions of Their DNA into RNA Molecules 5   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Cells Use Proteins as Catalysts 6   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Cells Translate RNA into Protein in the Same Way 6   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Each Protein Is Encoded by a Specific Gene 7   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Life Requires a Continual Input of Free Energy 7   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Cells Function as Biochemical Factories 8   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Cells Are Enclosed in a Plasma Membrane Across Which Nutrients and Waste Materials Must Pass 8   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Operate at a Microscopic Scale Dominated by Random Thermal Motion 9   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Living Cell Can Exist with 500 Genes 10   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 10   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "GENOME DIVERSIFICATION AND THE TREE OF LIFE 10   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Tree of Life Has Three Major Domains: Eukaryotes, Bacteria, and Archaea 11   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotes Make Up the Domain of Life That Is Most Familiar to Us 13   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "On the Basis of Genome Analysis, Bacteria Are the Most Diverse Group of Organisms on the Planet 13   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Archaea: The Most Mysterious Domain of Life 15   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Organisms Occupy Most of Our Planet 15   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Can Be Powered by a Wide Variety of Free-Energy Sources 15   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Cells Fix Nitrogen and Carbon Dioxide for Other Cells 17   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Genomes Diversify Over Evolutionary Time, Producing New Types of Organisms 18   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "New Genes Are Generated from Preexisting Genes 19   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Gene Duplications Give Rise to Families of Related Genes Within a Single Genome 20   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Function of a Gene Can Often Be Deduced from Its Nucleotide Sequence 20   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "More Than 200 Gene Families Are Common to All Three Domains of Life 21   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 21   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "EUKARYOTES AND THE ORIGIN OF THE EUKARYOTIC CELL 22   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Cells Contain a Variety of Organelles 23   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mitochondria Evolved from a Symbiotic Bacterium Captured by an Ancient Archaeon 25   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chloroplasts Evolved from a Symbiotic Photosynthetic Bacterium Engulfed by an Ancient Eukaryotic Cell 26   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotes Have Hybrid Genomes 27   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Genomes Are Big 28   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Genomes Are Rich in Regulatory DNA 28   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Eukaryotic Genomes Define the Program of Multicellular Development 29   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Eukaryotes Live as Solitary Cells 30   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 31   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "MODEL ORGANISMS 31   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mutations Reveal the Functions of Genes 32   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Molecular Biology Began with a Spotlight on One Bacterium and Its Viruses 33   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Focus on E. coli as a Model Organism Has Accelerated Many Subsequent Discoveries 35   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Yeast Serves as a Minimal Model Eukaryote 36"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
